repository-code: 'https://github.com/lucasguesserts/sclp-heuristic'
abstract: >-
  The three-dimensional single container loading problem aims to fill a container
  with items to maximize the volume usage. Items are rectangular and may rotate,
  but only orthogonal placements are allowed. We consider a beam search heuristic
  embedded into a double-effort mechanism. It uses a cover representation with
  maximal empty spaces for the residual space and generation of blocks of items
  in a guillotine pattern. A two-stage fitness function selects the blocks, and a
  greedy search selects the most promising partial packings. We propose many
  advancements on this heuristic: specific data structures representing residual
  spaces; a deeper calibration of its metaparameters; memoization to avoid
  recomputation of packings; and a two-stage fitness function. Computational
  experiments on the 1,600 BR benchmark instances demonstrate the high performance
  of the proposed method. Under identical hardware, our method achieves 95.91%
  average volume usage with 30 seconds, a +0.59% improvement over the
  state-of-the-art with 30 seconds (95.31%). With 5 seconds, it achieves a 95.40%
  result, still outperforming the state-of-the-art by +0.08%. This represents a
  major advance when contextualized historically: the method of 2014 required
  500 seconds and achieved a +0.42% gain while the one of 2017 reduced runtime
  to 30 seconds and achieved a +0.13% gain.
keywords:
  - combinatorial optimization
  - heuristics
  - tree search
  - load planing
  - 3D packing
  - container loading problem
